### Expected behavior

**Expected.** Compaction memory bounded by the entries since the latest compaction boundary (or the configured window), not by the session's lifetime: walk the branch in pages, or keep it off the main thread.

### Actual behavior

**Results (main-thread heap, 1-second samples)**

| session | events | heap before | during compaction (about 3 min) | after |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 575 MB | 22,273 | 225 MB | about 870 MB (large-object space +510 MB) | about 250 MB |
| 50 MB | 1,943 | 218 MB | 339 MB | about 250 MB |

A main-thread heap snapshot taken mid-compaction (814 MB used) holds one `SessionManager`. Its `byId` Map reaches 579 MB (570 MB of it strings, 287k objects): the whole transcript. Retaining path: `embeddedAgentBaseStreamFnCache` (WeakMap) → `AgentSession` → `streamFn` closure context → `sessionManager` → `SessionManager.byId`.

**Seen in production first** (a Telegram group session with 38,296 events, 575 MB of event JSON, 38,293 of them on the active path; gateway's own `diagnostics.stability` ring, 30-second samples):

| time (UTC) | event | main heap used | RSS |
|---|---|---|---|
| 17:31:29 | before | 1267 MB | 4372 MB |
| 17:31:47 | message arrives; preflight compaction begins (no runner phases, no log lines for 12 s) | | |
| 17:31:59 | | 2108 MB | 5334 MB |
| 17:32:02 | summary call starts (request 171 KB) | | |
| 17:32:29 | | 2426 MB | 5416 MB |
| 17:34:12 | `Compaction safeguard: finalized artifact truncated` | | |
| 17:34:29 | | 2614 MB | 6019 MB |
| 17:34:35 | turn ends | | |
| 17:34:59 | after | 1294 MB | 5359 MB |

Worker heaps stayed at about 1.1 GB throughout. Turns in the same session with 0.5–1.06 MB requests and no compaction left the heap at about 1.2 GB. All six safeguard compactions that day sit next to the day's largest RSS samples. The production rise is larger than the off-box one, likely because the production session has 38,296 events against the seed's 22,273.

**Where the code loads it (10.9 dist)**
- `readPreflightTranscriptContextMessages` opens the session with `SessionManager.openModelContextAsync` and calls `buildSessionContext()` (agent-runner-memory chunk).
- `compactEmbeddedAgentSession` → `prepareCompaction(this.sessionManager.getBranch(), …)` walks every entry on the branch, and so do `buildSessionContext(pathEntries)` for the retention budget and the safeguard extension's `projectBranchEntries(readSessionBranch(…))`.

### OpenClaw version

2026.10.9

### Operating system

Linux x64 (Ubuntu 24.04), Node 24.22.1

### Install method

npm (openclaw 2026.10.9)

### Model

Mock OpenAI-compatible model (fixture/fixture; compaction summary model fixture/summ in the compaction repro)

### Provider / routing chain

openclaw -> local mock OpenAI-compatible server (api: openai-completions)

### Additional provider/model setup details

_No response_

### Logs

```shell

```

### Screenshots, recordings, and evidence

_No response_

### Impact and severity

**Takeaways**
- We learned that on 2026.10.9 a compaction holds the whole parsed transcript in the main-thread heap for as long as the summary takes. A 575 MB session added about 650 MB off-box and 0.8–1.35 GB on a production gateway, for about 3 minutes each time. A 50 MB session added about 120 MB, so the cost scales with the session's lifetime, not with what is being summarized.
- You should bound compaction memory by the entries since the last compaction boundary (or page the branch walk) instead of walking every entry the session has ever had.
- Until then, users with long-lived chat sessions (busy Telegram groups) can cap it by starting a fresh session. Memory guards that watch RSS will read each compaction as a leak step, because RSS falls back far more slowly than the heap.

### Additional information

**Version:** OpenClaw 2026.10.9 (Linux, Node 24.22). Not yet checked on 2026.10.10 or later.

**Also worth checking:** with `compaction.maxActiveTranscriptBytes: "2mb"`, a session whose active path holds hundreds of MB can never get under the limit by compacting; only the byte-compaction latch stops it from compacting before every turn.

Disclosure: OpenAI is now the key benefactor of OpenClaw, and OpenClaw's creator, Peter Steinberger, works for OpenAI. OpenAI began through YC Research, which was started by Sam Altman, a former president of Y Combinator. Garry Tan runs Y Combinator.

### Bug type
Behavior bug (incorrect state without a crash). Not a beta release blocker.

### Summary
When deleting a Telegram draft preview fails, the adapter logs a warning but forgets which message still needs deletion. A second `stream.clear()` does not retry, even if the API is now healthy. The temporary progress message can remain next to an otherwise successfully delivered final reply.

### Steps to reproduce
No bot token, model, subagent, or live chat is needed. This regression case uses the existing helpers in [`extensions/telegram/src/draft-stream.test.ts`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/9618c4116960556e5519dd4c3eee404423a93318/extensions/telegram/src/draft-stream.test.ts):

```ts
it("retries the same preview after a transient delete failure", async () => {
  await withFakeTimers(async () => {
    const api = createMockDraftApi(); // sends preview 17 to fixture chat 123
    api.deleteMessage.mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error("temporary failure")); // subsequent calls succeed
    const stream = createDraftStream(api);
    await updateAndFlush(stream, "Test preview");
    await vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync(6_000); // beyond the minimum preview dwell
    await stream.clear();
    await vi.advanceTimersByTimeAsync(0); // let the detached request settle
    await stream.clear();
    await vi.runAllTimersAsync();
    expect(api.deleteMessage.mock.calls).toEqual([[123, 17], [123, 17]]);
  });
});
```

### Expected behavior
A transient failure leaves that exact preview eligible for a bounded cleanup retry. A confirmed successful delete is not repeated. Retrying an old preview must neither block final delivery nor delete a newer preview.

### Actual behavior and evidence
Ran the adapter from the installed `2026.11.3` distribution in an isolated Node process with a mock Bot API and the dwell period elapsed. Two calls to `clear()` produced:

| First delete result (next call would succeed) | Delete calls | Warnings |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Rejected promise | `[[123, 17]]` | 1 |
| Resolved `false` | `[[123, 17]]` | 1 |
| Resolved `true` (control) | `[[123, 17]]` | 0 |

On [upstream `main` at `9618c41`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/9618c4116960556e5519dd4c3eee404423a93318/extensions/telegram/src/draft-stream.ts#L677-L695), `clear()` resets `streamMessageId` before scheduling the delete. The [detached handler](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/9618c4116960556e5519dd4c3eee404423a93318/extensions/telegram/src/draft-stream.ts#L629-L660) warns on `false` or rejection but does not retain the ID for another attempt. The issue is not the intentional dwell or the fact that deletion is asynchronous.

### Environment
OpenClaw `2026.11.3` (runtime reproduction); upstream source checked at the pinned commit above. Linux x64, Node.js 26.13.1. Model/provider: not involved; Telegram outbound preview cleanup only.

Related: #104865 describes the analogous failure on Discord. #155641 addressed cleanup ownership in other adapters; Telegram still uses its own detached-delete path.

