## What Problem This Solves

Pressing ↑ in a long conversation stops at the oldest prompt in the currently loaded transcript. Reusing an earlier prompt requires manually loading older messages first.

## User Impact

↑ can fetch older prompts on demand without loading them into the visible conversation. ↓ restores the unsent draft and its mentions. The existing 100-prompt limit and caret/modifier/IME behavior are preserved. No configuration or migration is required.

## Why This Change Was Made

Extend the existing prompt-recall snapshot through the existing read-only history paging API. Keep active traversal order stable, leave transcript and scroll state alone, and reject delayed results after edits, navigation, or connection/session changes. History read failures use the existing visible error surface.

## Evidence

### Real isolated Gateway acceptance proof

Tested prepared candidate `6fee08a8abaa87f08dae4df5366be49eeca8c371` with the compiled Gateway and production-built Control UI. The served UI asset was checked byte-for-byte against the build output. The session was created through the real Gateway RPC; 282 synthetic input messages were persisted through the canonical transcript owner. No Gateway responses, browser cache, or UI state were mocked or replaced.

- Initial real history response: 80 messages, including “Latest prompt” but not “Recent prompt” or “Earlier prompt.”
- The second ↑ issued exactly one real `chat.history` cursor request. Its 202-message response contained the two older prompts; subsequent ↑ recalled both.
- ↓ walked forward and restored “Keep this unsent follow-up.” Caret positions were checked after every key.
- Visible transcript text and row identities stayed unchanged. Scroll position remained exactly `7920`; the visible anchor stayed at the same viewport position.
- The restored-draft PNG is byte-identical to the initial PNG. The owned Gateway and browser were shut down; no fixture Gateway process remained.

This is a history-only flow: the configured synthetic provider was unused, and no model inference was performed. The filtered acceptance case was task-owned and removed afterward; this evidence-only update adds no production code or permanent test inventory.

