## What Problem This Solves

Keeps a later LINE batch rejection from dropping valid reply parts after an earlier batch was accepted.

Fixes #142734

## Why This Change Was Made

Mixed LINE reply parts stay ordered and use batches of up to five. When a batch gets definitive HTTP 400, each part is retried individually before accepted receipts are combined; failures remain visible in the partial receipt. Recovery deduplicates receipt IDs already recorded for accepted sends, including cumulative receipts from a throwing delivery observer.

When the batch and every individual retry are explicitly refused, the adapter preserves all refusal evidence in a permanent no-send error so durable delivery can retire it. If any recovery attempt has an ambiguous transport failure, the aggregate stays unproven.

## User Impact

Mixed replies use fewer provider requests; a rejected later batch no longer drops valid text, and callers receive every accepted message ID without replaying an accepted batch.

## Evidence

- At exact PR HEAD `c6e6ba099e8a23b5bdbbe6979d78812c5b15bf98` (rebase target `f3345c6e879477adde36f0938d5b9733faca1ca0`), the production-path harness sends mixed Flex, location, and text in one ordered POST. Plain-text and media-only controls each use one POST. Proof source SHA256: `d6dec0696411b727c3767a30eae0f0659460837e76571127cb6b7417fc951290`; captured output SHA256: `42d5e8ccfdb7b00aa4d4a78dc0bbf190ce70f8f95a5bef82cd50a0088a12f5ea`.
- Later-batch fault at exact HEAD: the first five-message POST returns 200; the later text+image POST returns 400; text retry returns 200 and image retry returns 400. The partial receipt contains six accepted IDs (the first five plus recovered text), with no replay of the first batch.
- Observer-failure fault at exact HEAD: after a mixed Flex+text batch returns 400, Flex retry returns 200 and its delivery observer throws; text retry still returns 200. Final `receipt.parts` and `messageIds` each contain exactly the two accepted IDs.
- Recovery fault at exact HEAD: the mixed batch returns 400, then Flex, location, and text singleton attempts return 200, 400, and 200. The partial receipt contains both accepted IDs.
- Unit regressions verify all singleton HTTP 400 refusals remain typed permanent no-send evidence, an `ECONNRESET` recovery remains unproven, and accepted receipts are preserved once. Core queue policy tests confirm only proven permanent rejection is terminal.
- Invalid media preparation and malformed HTTP 200 receipt controls preserve accepted IDs. Exact-head focused LINE suites passed 48/48 tests across four files after removing the redundant mock-only receipt test; durable delivery queue policy tests passed 5/5. The loopback harness redirects only LINE API push requests and makes no request to LINE or the supplied media URL.
- Contract: [LINE Messaging API: Sending messages](https://developers.line.biz/en/docs/messaging-api/sending-messages/) accepts up to five message objects per request.

