AI-assisted: built with Codex

Closes #199251

## What Problem This Solves

When several agent runs share one API key and one of them hits the provider's tokens-per-minute limit, the other runs are supposed to wait until the limit resets. A run that was already sending a request when the limit was learned can still resend inside that same request (for example after a rejected reasoning replay or a fallback from WebSocket to HTTP). That resend goes out early and gets its own 429.

## User Impact

User impact: agents running many conversations on one inline or SecretRef API key wait for a known rate-limit reset instead of spending extra requests on more 429s, and the wait shows the usual rate-limit status. Nothing changes when no 429 was seen.

## Why This Change Was Made

New requests already wait for a learned rate-limit deadline before they start. A resend inside a request that already started never checked that shared limiter. The guarded model fetch, which every physical provider send goes through, now asks the same limiter before it sends. If a deadline for the same model and credential is still in the future, the send waits in the existing queue with the call's retry cap, priority and status reporting, the same inputs the start of the request uses.

- No deadline, or one that already passed (the normal first send), adds no wait.
- A deadline beyond the call's retry cap or request timeout sends as before, so the provider's own 429 still drives recovery.
- The wait follows the request's cancel signal, so a cancelled run sends nothing.

Accepted edges:
- A deadline that lands just under a short, non-default request timeout can leave the resend too little time and end as a timeout.
- Credentials sent only in a non-Authorization header (for example `x-api-key`) are not matched at the physical send. Those requests skip the wait, as today. The affected Responses path sends `Authorization: Bearer`.

## Evidence

- Real HTTP run against a local stub provider, before and after (below): on main, the in-flight run resends 2.7 s before the shared deadline and gets a second 429. With this change it waits, sends 15 ms after the deadline and gets 200.
- New tests in `src/agents/provider-transport-fetch.test.ts` fail on main and pass here: a resend waits for a deadline learned after the request started while another key sends at once; the call's retry cap and timeout decide whether a 90 s deadline is held; a resend cancelled during the wait sends nothing. With the production change reverted (`git apply -R`), the same tests fail again.
- Consumer suites pass (11 files, listed below), and `pnpm check:changed` against a freshly fetched `origin/main` passes every lane except the dead export scan. That scan flags `SubagentRequesterWakeProgressChangedError` in `src/agents/subagents/completion/subagent-completion-admission.store.ts`, a file this PR does not touch; it is already flagged on main `2d52a33ef7a`.
- Local review: ClawSweeper `--local-range` (Claude runner), base `2d52a33ef7a`, head `c3648eda28e`, full committed range, review complete with checkout access verified. Outcome: patch is correct (confidence 0.8), no findings, security cleared; rating C (proof D, patch B). The proof rating covers only the committed tests, not the runtime output below.
