Root cause: src/gateway/server-node-events-notifications.ts:128 admits phone notifications through `enqueueSessionEventForHost` without the producer's `replyExpectation: "optional"`, so in a WebChat session the `internal_system` turn resolves as result-required (src/agents/reply-completion.ts:116-122) and the CLI runner rejects an authored `NO_REPLY` as `empty_response`, which starts model fallback.

Fixes #200710

Thanks @dawidkulpa for the report with exact before/after counts, and @vaibhav375 for the fix.

## What Problem This Solves

Since 2026.11.5, when an agent decides a forwarded phone notification needs no answer and replies `NO_REPLY`, the turn fails with "CLI backend returned an empty response." and OpenClaw retries it on the fallback model. With a subscription CLI backend and an API-billed fallback, every ignored notification becomes a paid request and an "Unable to finish this reply" card in WebChat.

## User Impact

User impact: agents can ignore phone notifications again. A `NO_REPLY` answer ends the turn silently, with no error card and no fallback model call. Notifications the agent does answer still get their reply.

## Why This Change Was Made

#180106 (e897d98dcc2a) moved notifications from heartbeat wakes, which allowed silence, onto ordinary host session events, without carrying over that permission. The notification producer now passes `replyExpectation: "optional"`, the same producer-owned waiver cron notifications and session-state notices already pass. Input provenance alone still grants no silence, so other `internal_system` turns keep their result requirement.

The branch keeps the contributor's fix commit. The in-process Gateway test and the docs note are folded into one assertion in the existing notification routing case, which fails on `main` and passes here.

## Evidence

Isolated source Gateway (`ocx src`), loopback, throwaway state. The agent's primary and fallback models are a scripted local CLI backend that logs every call. A WebChat client sends `chat.send` to `agent:main:main`, then a paired `openclaw-android` node sends `notifications.changed` for a promo (model answers `NO_REPLY`) and for a bank alert (model answers with text, the control).

