## What Problem This Solves

Fixes guided channel setup staying busy on every device after its starting connection closes, including when the first `wizard.start` reply was lost.

## User Impact

After reconnecting, users can start channel setup again once any in-progress write and cleanup finish, without an additional idle-expiry wait. Completed installs and saved settings remain. Full onboarding keeps its existing reconnect behavior.

Maintainer sign-off is requested for ending channel setup on any starting-socket disconnect, including a temporary network interruption.

This addresses abandoned setup admission. It does not remove synchronous plugin-capture stalls or the resulting silence-probe timeout; that separate work remains tracked in #160959 and #176192.

## Why This Change Was Made

Channel setup opts into the existing Gateway wizard disconnect cleanup. The shared owner closes input with `WizardSession.close()`, preserves protected writes, and releases admission only after settlement. Credential-login ownership stays connection-bound; channel-session access rules stay as before.

Thanks to @masatohoshino for the earlier work in closed, unmerged #170996. This takes a different approach to the channel case: use the transport lifetime instead of introducing its proposed 25-minute idle deadline.

## Evidence

- Packaged candidate proof also passed through the standard CLI entry point and stock Discord runner. A transport proxy dropped the first start reply; a competing start was busy until that client disconnected. Replacement setup then succeeded. Disconnecting again at the real **Discord account** prompt also allowed a fresh setup. The sanitized terminal trace is below.
- Negative control on unchanged main `2435c1faa791de766688ba27ca7a14a92e30b7e4`: disconnecting the starting client never reached cleanup (`abandoned before start reply did not close`). After the fix, the same lost-reply, pending-prompt, and protected-write scenarios pass. The final regression uses the test cancellation signal instead of adding a wall-clock timeout.
- All 8 real-Gateway checks passed across `gateway-wizard-cancel.e2e.test.ts` and `gateway-wizard.e2e.test.ts`, covering onboarding rejoin, shutdown, consent, and retained operator authority. After the wait-helper correction, all 4 cancellation-file checks passed again: 196.66 s total including source preparation; 60.35 s in the file, 28.85 s in the new regression. Real WebSocket and process-owned admission/cleanup are the required proof boundary; its three disconnect scenarios share one Gateway. CI seconds are pending.
- All 62 focused wizard, admission, authority, and model-login checks passed (183.06 s including preparation). The unchanged session suite also passed 33 checks on the base.
- `pnpm build:ci-artifacts` passed for production commit `3ea29b79839eae685ed5175e1c2ddb2dce3a8225`; the follow-up changes only the test. Targeted production/test lint, formatting, Gateway-root test typechecking, `git diff --check`, and the timeout-race ratchet passed. No baseline allowance was increased.
- Local autoreview at P0–P2 found no actionable issues in the complete candidate against the base above. A follow-up review of the test-only adjustment, supplied with the wait helper and Gateway teardown implementations, also found no actionable issues. An unused test initializer was removed after the successful test run; its assignments and assertions are unchanged.
- Simplification: reuse the existing lifecycle owner; no new expiry timer or session registry. Production +12/-5 lines, net test growth 99 lines, docs +7.
- [Upstream CI](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/43973611303) awaits maintainer approval to run the fork workflows.

One intervening test preparation was stopped by the harness watchdog during a confirmed host sleep, before assertions ran. The focused rerun afterward passed as reported above.

