Closes #146117

## What Problem This Solves

People often send several WhatsApp screenshots or photos followed by one instruction such as “compare these.” OpenClaw currently preserves the attachments but dispatches each image as a separate agent turn, which can produce fragmented or premature replies.

## Why This Change Was Made

This adds an opt-in `channels.whatsapp.batchInboundImages` setting. When enabled, rapid images and accompanying text from the same sender and conversation use the configured WhatsApp inbound debounce window and are delivered as one ordered multimodal turn. The default remains unchanged; video, audio, documents, locations, replies/quotes, and control commands still dispatch immediately.

The durable ingress lane is released only for accounts that enable the feature so later images can join the same deferred batch without weakening the default lane-serialization behavior.

## User Impact

Operators can enable coherent multi-image WhatsApp requests without losing image order or read receipts:

```json5
{
  channels: {
    whatsapp: {
      batchInboundImages: true,
    },
  },
  messages: {
    inbound: {
      byChannel: {
        whatsapp: 2000,
      },
    },
  },
}
```

Users who do not enable the option see no behavior change.

## Review Follow-up

- Explicit `media.kind` is authoritative: JPEG documents and WebP stickers stay on immediate dispatch even when their MIME type starts with `image/`.
- Image-containing batches preserve durable receive order even when provider timestamps arrive out of order; timestamp-first ordering remains unchanged for text-only batches.
- Added regressions for both contracts.

## Evidence

- Full WhatsApp extension suite after rebasing onto current `main`: 1,419 tests passed.
- Focused auto-reply E2E suite: 29 tests passed.
- Added integration coverage proving two durable rapid image messages become one ordered payload with both read receipts.
- Added schema, account inheritance, debounce aggregation, canonical media projection, command bypass, captionless image, explicit non-image-kind, receive-order, and non-image immediate-dispatch coverage.
- `pnpm check:test-types`, `pnpm check:docs`, and `pnpm check:changed` passed.

The repository autoreview helper could not run because the standalone `codex` executable is not installed on the contributor machine; its bundle preflight succeeded and the full diff was reviewed manually after all required checks passed.

Real linked-account proof is still pending; the evidence above is repository integration/E2E proof and is not presented as a production WhatsApp capture.

issues.body:
**Files:** `container/cli-tools.test.ts`, `.claude/skills/add-vercel/vercel-manifest.test.ts`

`container/cli-tools.test.ts` has:

```ts
it('bakes in nothing that a skill is meant to add on request', () => {
  const names = manifest.map((t) => t.name);
  expect(names).not.toContain('vercel');
});
```

`/add-vercel` installs the CLI by appending `{ "name": "vercel", "version": … }` to the same `container/cli-tools.json`. Its own `vercel-manifest.test.ts` then asserts `expect(names).toContain('vercel')`.

Once the skill is applied, the trunk suite and the skill's suite contradict each other, so `pnpm test` goes red on any install that has `/add-vercel`. The guard is meant to stop vercel being baked in by default. But it can't tell "trunk ships vercel" apart from "the operator installed vercel via the sanctioned skill", because both are the same manifest entry.

**Repro:** run `/add-vercel` on a clean checkout, then `pnpm exec vitest run container/cli-tools.test.ts`.

**Suggested fixes:**
- Run the boundary check against the manifest as shipped on trunk, not the working copy. For example, compare against a baseline list, or mark skill-added entries (`"addedBy": "add-vercel"`) and exclude those.
- Or drop the guard and rely on review. The opt-in boundary is already documented.

## QwenPaw Version

Upstream `main` @ `28f00a679c` (v2.3.0.post1, 2026-11-17). The behavior has been present since `c54a4465` ("fix(console): adapt mobile chat input for safe area and virtual keyboard"), which introduced `viewport-fit=cover`.

**Related issue:** #4738 — that issue fixed the **bottom** safe area only. This report is the missing **top** counterpart.

**Security considerations:** None.

## Component(s) Affected

- [ ] Core / Backend (app, agents, config, providers, utils, local_models)
- [x] Console (frontend web UI)
- [ ] Channels (DingTalk, Feishu, QQ, Discord, iMessage, etc.)
- [ ] Skills
- [ ] CLI
- [ ] Documentation (website)
- [ ] Tests
- [ ] CI/CD
- [ ] Scripts / Deploy

## Description

When the Web Console is opened in a **full-screen container** (a WebView without browser chrome, a PWA installed in `standalone` mode, or an iOS/Android home-screen shortcut), the top of the page is drawn underneath the system status bar / notch. The status bar clock and battery icons overlap the page content, and the first row of UI becomes partially unreadable and untappable.

`console/index.html` declares:

```html
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0, viewport-fit=cover" />
```

With `viewport-fit=cover`, the layout viewport is extended into the notch / status bar area, and the page is expected to inset its own content using `env(safe-area-inset-*)`. The console does this for the **bottom** edge but not for the **top** edge.

Verified on upstream `main` today:

```
$ git grep -n "safe-area-inset-top" -- console/
(no matches)

$ git grep -n "safe-area-inset" -- console/
console/src/components/LoopInput/index.module.less:271:  padding: 10px 12px calc(12px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
console/src/components/interaction/BottomSheet.module.less:62:  max(var(--app-space-5), env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
console/src/components/interaction/BottomSheet.module.less:71:  max(var(--app-space-5), env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
console/src/features/project-directory/SessionProjectDirectory.module.less:879: ... env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
console/src/features/terminal/TerminalDock.module.less:130: ... max(12px, env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
console/src/layouts/index.module.less:1154:  padding-bottom: max(8px, env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
console/src/os/useOsStyles.ts:1216:  bottom: max(8px, env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
console/src/os/useOsStyles.ts:1531:  bottom: max(8px, env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
console/src/pages/Chat/components/ApprovalToggle.module.less:5: ... env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px));
console/src/pages/Chat/index.module.less:411: ... env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px)) !important;
console/src/pages/CommunityFeedback/index.module.less:609:  padding: 12px 16px max(12px, env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
console/src/pages/CommunityFeedback/index.module.less:885:  padding: 12px 16px max(12px, env(safe-area-inset-bottom));
console/src/styles/layout.css:892:  padding-bottom: env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px) !important;
console/src/styles/layout.css:911:  padding-bottom: calc(env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px) + 8px) !important;
```

**17 occurrences, all `-bottom`, zero `-top`.** The top edge is never handled.

Additionally, 15 "mobile adaptation" PRs (#5444, #5446, #5451, #5369, #5384, #5385, #5391, #5397, #5445, #5449, #5458, #5459, #5463, #5464, #5470) adjust layout via responsive breakpoints (`max-width: 768px`) but none of them address safe-area insets. `#5444` ("mobile adaptation for sidebar, header, and page header"), the most relevant one, contains no `safe-area` or `inset-top` at all.

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Deploy the console and open it on a phone **in a full-screen container**, for example:
   - an Android app embedding the console in a `WebView` (`setEdgeToEdgeEnabled`/immersive mode without `WindowInsets` padding), **or**
   - a PWA added to the home screen (`display: standalone`), **or**
   - an Android/iOS home-screen shortcut opening in standalone mode.
2. Observe the top 0–50 px of the page, i.e. `layouts/index.module.less` `.mainLayout` (line 2, `height: 100vh`), which contains `.header` (line 45, `height: 56px`, no top inset) and `.sider` (line 101).
3. Compare with the status bar overlay: the clock and battery icons sit on top of the header content.

Note: the bug is **not** reproducible in a normal mobile browser tab, because the browser chrome occupies the status bar region and `env(safe-area-inset-top)` resolves to `0px` there. It only appears once the browser chrome disappears (WebView / standalone).

## Actual vs Expected

- **Actual:** The page starts at the physical top edge of the screen. `.mainLayout` has `height: 100vh` with no `padding-top`, so the header / sidebar brand is rendered underneath the system status bar and is partially obscured by it.
- **Expected:** The page should reserve `env(safe-area-inset-top)` at the top edge, keeping the background color bleeding behind the status bar (so the notch area is themed) while the interactive content starts below the status bar — consistent with how the 17 existing `-bottom` insets already behave.

## Suggested Fix

Apply the top inset on the root layout container, which fixes every page at once:

```diff
 /* console/src/layouts/index.module.less */
 .mainLayout {
   height: 100vh;
   min-height: 100vh;
   flex-direction: row;
   overflow: hidden;
+  box-sizing: border-box;
+  padding-top: env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px);
+  padding-left: env(safe-area-inset-left, 0px);
+  padding-right: env(safe-area-inset-right, 0px);
 }
```

And the equivalent for the desktop-OS shell (`console/src/os/useOsStyles.ts`), where `MENUBAR_H` is currently a flat 28 px:

```diff
-  height: MENUBAR_H,
+  height: calc(MENUBAR_Hpx + env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px)),
+  paddingTop: "env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px)",
```

The `Login` page (`console/src/pages/Login/index.tsx`) and the full-screen chat view also use `100vh` roots, so the inline `padding` there needs the same treatment:

```diff
-  padding: "24px 16px",
+  padding: "calc(24px + env(safe-area-inset-top, 0px)) 16px calc(24px + env(safe-area-inset-bottom, 0px))",
```

Because `env(safe-area-inset-top)` resolves to `0px` on desktop and in normal browser tabs, these changes are no-ops outside full-screen mobile containers, so they carry no regression risk.

A `100dvh`-based height alongside `100vh` would also be worth adding for the mobile browser toolbar case:

```less
@supports (height: 100dvh) {
  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .mainLayout { height: 100dvh; min-height: 100dvh; }
  }
}
```

## Logs / Screenshots

Measured from a phone screenshot captured in a full-screen container (device pixel ratio ≈ 3.1, screenshot 1280 × 2781 physical px):

- The first non-background page pixels appear at physical `y ≈ 60 px` (≈ 19 CSS px from the top edge), i.e. **above** the status bar baseline.
- The status bar icon band occupies physical `y ≈ 80–118 px` (clock, battery).
- The page's own top-row elements occupy the same `y ≈ 75–118 px` band, so the two overlap directly.
- Sampled colors in the overlapped region match the console brand logo gradient (`#15D1EE` → `#246BFF`), confirming that it is a page-owned element rather than a browser overlay.

I can attach the raw screenshot as a follow-up comment if that helps — GitHub's REST API cannot upload binary attachments, so this report uses pixel measurements instead.

## Additional Notes

This is a general Web-platform defect of the console, not specific to any downstream customisation. Since `viewport-fit=cover` is opt-in and already enabled, adding the matching top inset completes the intent of #4738 rather than changing the design. Happy to send a PR against `.mainLayout` + `.header` + `useOsStyles.ts` if the maintainers agree with the approach.

As far as I can tell, when a headless SOP step calls `send_message_to_peer`, the recipient agent's turn runs without the step's tool scope: the scope is carried in a task-local, and the tool starts the recipient's turn in a detached task without capturing it. The sibling tools `delegate` and `spawn_subagent` do carry the scope into the turns they start.

### Affected component

tools

### Severity

S2 - degraded behavior

### Current behavior

A headless SOP step runs through `agent::run` with the scope in `AgentRunOverrides` and also publishes the same scope as a task-local: `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/sop/executor.rs:1063` (anchor `sop_step_scope: Some(scope),`) and `:1083` (anchor `with_active_headless_step_scope(`). The turn loop re-reads the task-local on every iteration and merges the step's exclusions into the turn's excluded tools (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/turn/mod.rs:1916`, anchor `active_headless_step_scope().map(|scope| {`).

In `send_message_to_peer`, the in-process branch starts the recipient's turn in a detached task:

- `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/send_message_to_peer.rs:333`, anchor `zeroclaw_spawn::spawn!(async move {`
- `:336`, anchor `crate::agent::loop_::process_message_shared_with_live_config_and_admission_and_principal(`, with `TurnOrigin::AgentDirect` (`:342`) and `InternalPrincipal::PeerAgent` (`:344`).

A task-local does not cross a spawn; `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/sop/active_scope.rs:82-86` says so (anchor `A task-local does not cross \`spawn\``). The same block already works around this for the cost context (`send_message_to_peer.rs:321-326`, anchor `a detached \`zeroclaw_spawn::spawn!\` task does not inherit the caller's`), but nothing captures the step scope. Searching `send_message_to_peer.rs` for `headless_step_scope|sop_step_scope|active_scope` gives 0 matches, while the same file does match `spawn!` at `:333`, so the search sees the file.

The entry point the tool calls has no parameter for a scope (`process_message_inner`, `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/loop_.rs:3613-3622`), and it builds the recipient's registry itself with `caller_allowed: None` (`loop_.rs:3788`). Inside the recipient's turn, `active_headless_step_scope()` returns `None` (`active_scope.rs:105-107`). So the recipient is offered its own tool surface (45 tools in the run below), not the step's.

The sibling tools do carry it:
- `delegate`: captures before the spawn and restores inside it (`delegate.rs:3377` / `:3416` for background delegation, `:3717` / `:3837` for parallel fan-out), and filters the target's registry (`delegate.rs:5059-5073`, anchor `Handing work to another agent is not a way to`, a comment that continues on the next line).
- `spawn_subagent`: `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/spawn_subagent.rs:98`, anchor `sop_step_scope: crate::sop::active_scope::active_headless_step_scope(),`.

When the SOP runtime is enabled, the recipient's turn also builds its own SOP engine (`loop_.rs:3737`, anchor `if config.sop.runtime_enabled() {`) and `all_tools_with_runtime...` registers `sop_execute`, `sop_advance` and `sop_approve` when an engine is present (`crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/mod.rs:2717-2756`). The step turn always drops those three (`active_scope.rs:11`, `SOP_CONTROL_TOOLS`). I did not run that part: my reproduction below does not enable the SOP runtime, so the control tools do not appear in it.

### Expected behavior

The code says a run started from inside a headless step keeps the step's boundary:

- `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/sop/active_scope.rs:97-103`: "A tool that starts a child agent run passes this into the child's `AgentRunOverrides`: spawning a child does not widen the step's capability boundary".
- `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/loop_.rs:1430-1437` (doc of `sop_step_scope`): "Also `Some` on a child run started from inside such a step, which inherits the step's boundary rather than rebuilding the agent's full surface."
- `crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/agent/turn/mod.rs:1909-1911`: reading the scope from the task "also covers the loops this one drives on behalf of another agent: a delegated target assembles its own registry, and inherits the boundary all the same."

The user docs make the same promise for child agents: `docs/book/src/sop/fan-in/cron.md:18` ("A child agent the step spawns inherits the same exclusions, so spawning one is not a way around the step's boundary") and `docs/book/src/sop/fan-in/overview.md:13`. They do not mention peers, so whether a peer turn counts as a "child agent" is a question for maintainers. `docs/book/src/developing/tool-inventory.md:68` groups `send_message_to_peer` with `spawn_subagent` and `delegate` as delegation that "must share risk profiles, tools, memory, and parent/child constraints". One line points the other way: `tool-inventory.md:209` lists what each of the three carries into the turn it starts, naming "its security policy and SOP step scope for `spawn_subagent`", delegation policy and ceilings for `delegate`, and "its identity as the peer turn's principal for `send_message_to_peer`". It does not name the step scope for `delegate` either, although `delegate` does carry it (`delegate.rs:3377`, `:3416`), so I read that line as not exhaustive. If carrying the step scope into peer turns is intentionally not part of the design, that is what I would like to know.

A minimal fix would be to capture `active_headless_step_scope()` before the `spawn!` in `send_message_to_peer.rs` and wrap the recipient's turn future with `with_inherited_headless_step_scope(...)`, as `delegate` does. I applied exactly that locally to check the test below: it then passes, and the recipient is offered only `send_message_to_peer`. I have not run any other test with it. Since open draft PRs (#11230, #11410, #11526) also edit this file, I would rather wait for maintainer guidance on where this should land before opening a PR.

### Steps to reproduce

```bash
# Tool-level reproduction on master @ 1c1cd118ac536a1dd1bc79b9cce69cbdc04e4f29.
# 1. Append the test below to `mod tests` in
#    crates/zeroclaw-runtime/src/tools/send_message_to_peer.rs
# 2. Run:
CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0 cargo test -p zeroclaw-runtime --lib --locked -j 1 \
  zz_peer_turn_in_headless_sop_step_scope_probe -- --test-threads=1 --nocapture
# Expected (contract): the recipient's request offers only `send_message_to_peer`.
# Observed: it offers 45 tools; 44 are outside the step's scope.
```

Setup the test uses: sender agent `sender` and recipient agent `recipient`, both in one peer group on `telegram.prod`; the recipient's provider is a local OpenAI-compatible mock that records the request bodies. The test installs a step scope (`allow = ["send_message_to_peer"]`, `step_scope_enforce = true`) as the task-local, the way the headless driver does (`executor.rs:1083`), calls the tool's `execute()` inside it, and then inspects the first request the recipient's turn sends to the mock. A precondition asserts that the request carried native tool specs at all.

```rust
    #[tokio::test]
    async fn zz_peer_turn_in_headless_sop_step_scope_probe() {
        use crate::control_plane::ControlPlaneHandle;
        use axum::{Json, Router, extract::State, routing::post};
        use std::collections::HashMap;
        use zeroclaw_config::schema::{
            ModelProviderConfig, OllamaModelProviderConfig, RiskProfileConfig, SopConfig,
        };

        type Bodies = Arc<Mutex<Vec<serde_json::Value>>>;
        async fn capture(
            State(bodies): State<Bodies>,
            Json(body): Json<serde_json::Value>,
        ) -> Json<serde_json::Value> {
            bodies.lock().push(body);
            Json(json!({
                "choices": [{"message": {"content": "ok"}}],
                "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 1, "completion_tokens": 1}
            }))
        }

        let bodies: Bodies = Arc::new(Mutex::new(Vec::new()));
        let listener = tokio::net::TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:0")
            .await
            .expect("bind");
        let addr = listener.local_addr().expect("addr");
        let app = Router::new()
            .route("/v1/chat/completions", post(capture))
            .with_state(bodies.clone());
        let server = zeroclaw_spawn::spawn!(async move {
            axum::serve(listener, app).await.expect("serve");
        });

        let workspace = tempfile::TempDir::new().expect("temp data dir");
        let mut config = Config {
            data_dir: workspace.path().to_path_buf(),
            config_path: workspace.path().join("config.toml"),
            ..Config::default()
        };
        config.providers.models.ollama.insert(
            "default".to_string(),
            OllamaModelProviderConfig {
                base: ModelProviderConfig {
                    model: Some("mock-model".to_string()),
                    uri: Some(format!("http://{addr}")),
                    timeout_secs: Some(5),
                    pricing: HashMap::new(),
                    ..ModelProviderConfig::default()
                },
                ..OllamaModelProviderConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.agents.insert(
            "sender".to_string(),
            AliasedAgentConfig {
                channels: vec!["telegram.prod".into()],
                ..AliasedAgentConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.agents.insert(
            "recipient".to_string(),
            AliasedAgentConfig {
                channels: vec!["telegram.prod".into()],
                model_provider: "ollama.default".into(),
                risk_profile: "default".into(),
                ..AliasedAgentConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config.peer_groups.insert(
            "ops".to_string(),
            PeerGroupConfig {
                channel: "telegram".into(),
                agents: vec![AgentAlias::new("sender"), AgentAlias::new("recipient")],
                ..PeerGroupConfig::default()
            },
        );
        config
            .risk_profiles
            .insert("default".to_string(), RiskProfileConfig::default());

        // The step may only message a peer; everything else is scoped out.
        let scope = crate::sop::active_scope::HeadlessStepScope {
            run_id: "run-1".into(),
            step: crate::sop::SopStep {
                number: 1,
                scope: Some(crate::sop::StepToolScope {
                    allow: Some(vec!["send_message_to_peer".into()]),
                    deny: Vec::new(),
                }),
                ..crate::sop::SopStep::default()
            },
            config: SopConfig {
                step_scope_enforce: true,
                ..SopConfig::default()
